@@ -124,3 +124,33 @@ error: missing prop `href` of `ButtonLinkProps`
   `test/jsx.test.ts`'s "a companion macro needs no docs in a crate that
   denies missing_docs" holds it, and that an item of the crate's own is
   still flagged.
+
+- **Ordinary wrappers of imported props use their owned companion**
+  (2026-11-14, #1794). The syntax pass previously knew only companions it
+  made in the current module, so a wrapper of imported or renamed React
+  attributes attempted a literal with nonexistent flattened fields.
+  Constructor generation now lives in jsx_syntax/constructors.rs. It records
+  unresolved ordinary-wrapper props by source identity. After expansion, Rust's
+  resolved HIR type identifies the struct; its defining module's macro must
+  carry the compiler-generated jsx_props marker. A same-name user macro is
+  insufficient. The driver replays parsing only when discovery finds a
+  companion that changes the constructor, carrying owned decisions rather
+  than rustc IDs between sessions; the replay checks those facts again.
+  Crates without a changed constructor continue in the original session.
+  Serde attributes are captured before the HIR query consumes the resolver
+  AST. Metadata, Clippy and lowering use the same syntax boundary. Imported
+  builders resolve in the hygienic component macro’s defining module. Rust
+  already protects them from lexical macro_rules shadows; a proposed explicit
+  self:: qualification was removed when its mutation survived. Plain structs, std::ops::Range and type
+  aliases without their own macro keep their existing literal construction.
+  A type alias itself does not create a new props companion.
+  Case N: JavaScript and native Rust observables are unchanged for previously
+  accepted programs; this lifts a JSX typing refusal. Native Rust's opaque
+  JSX placeholder cannot observe these fields, so SSR comparisons against
+  explicit expected markup and existing evaluation-order tests hold the
+  boundary. Compiler mutations cover the constructor, ownership marker,
+  resolution and literal fallback.
+
+  Remaining development-loop limitation: an early source warning may be
+  printed by both discovery and replay. This does not change runtime behavior
+  or bypass Rust diagnostics; diagnostic buffering remains follow-up work.
